**Current support.** An HTML entry is selected from implemented activity on a normal module root: a non-trivial root body, a direct const top-level fragment or a runtime fragment. A general `$entry` root purpose is deferred; the earlier `$page` proposal is superseded and is not implemented. Dependency roots remain non-activating.

Every selected normal module is compiled into an immutable module artefact. Its top-level runtime work becomes a dormant compiler-owned entry. Entry assembly activates that module-owned entry exactly once for a selected page. A dependency exposes declarations without activating its root.

### Current entry activity and future root purpose

The Alpha HTML builder selects a page from authored root activity. Any of these
makes a normal root an entry candidate:

- a non-trivial top-level runtime body
- a direct const top-level fragment
- a runtime top-level fragment

A direct named builder contribution such as a const root `$in title` is a const
fragment, so a root with only metadata still counts as output-producing
activity and emits a complete document.

The accepted future direction is a general `$entry` root purpose. It is deferred
and must not be used in source today. The earlier `$page` proposal is
superseded: `$page` is not an accepted directive and offers no metadata
arguments. `$entry` will make entry selection explicit while preserving
directory-derived routes, one page per normal root and dependency
non-activation. Future `$test` remains a separate non-page purpose. Neither
future purpose is a metadata path; initial document metadata belongs to builder
slots.

Only a direct top-level const `$in` on the active entry root reaches the
builder. A nested or aliased insert remains ordinary template composition, and
runtime top-level templates still contribute only to the default body lane.
Runtime named builder slots are unsettled final design and are not supported.

### Comment and documentation exemptions

Semantically classified `$doc`, `$note` and `$todo` templates are
documentation/comment processing. They do not create an HTML entry by
themselves. This exemption follows the template's semantic category, not
whether its rendered output happens to be empty.

Assigned or returned template values are ordinary values, not direct output.
Their containing runtime code follows the normal root placement rules.

### Entry-activated root runtime

The selected module root's top-level runtime code becomes a dormant
compiler-owned entry. Entry assembly activates it exactly
once. This includes top-level bindings, function calls and other runtime
statements. Helper files define reusable declarations. They do not own an
export block or an entry.

The entry has a built-in `Error!` contract. Checked numeric failure propagates to
that contract, and compatible top-level postfix `!` is legal. Custom typed
errors still need explicit recovery. A generated terminal handler closes the
entry boundary; an unhandled built-in failure does not become an implicit panic.
See @../progress (Progress) for compiler support.

### Page fragments

Two forms of direct top-level template in a module root become page fragments:

- A const fragment uses the `#` prefix before the opening bracket. It folds at compile time and is emitted as static HTML.
- A runtime fragment uses the plain bracket form without `#`. The module's entry evaluates it when entry assembly activates the module, but publication waits until that invocation succeeds.

The builder preserves source order between static and runtime fragments. Static
fragments remain in the document even when startup fails. Runtime fragments use
generated slots, and the entry boundary fills those slots only after successful
startup. Failure publishes none of that invocation's runtime fragments. This is
not rewind: other side effects do not roll back.

The generated terminal handler presents a generic safe release notice, not the
application's `Error` message as HTML. Development presentation, including the
three startup categories and invocation-local reporting, is owned by
`docs/build-system-design.md` > `Development entry-error reports` and summarised at
@../../developer-docs/compiler-design/html-assembly-and-output/#document-shell-and-fragments (HTML assembly).

### Metadata-only pages

A root with only direct const builder slot contributions still emits a complete
document. A page containing only resource-bearing metadata also emits the
resources it uses without non-empty executable HIR, an artificial fragment or
generated runtime code.

### Dependency roots

Dependency module roots never activate their root runtime. When a module is
bound for its public API, its top-level runtime code, entry body and page
fragments remain dormant. Only exported declarations remain available.

A consumer cannot make a dependency's root work or fragments part of its own
entry, and imported module roots do not contribute named builder slots to the
consumer. Valid dependency access and `check` remain non-activating.

### Do not put page fragments in helper files

Helper files should define reusable declarations. The module root should bind
and use them. Page-producing top-level templates belong in the selected module
root only. An ordinary file cannot become a page by containing a fragment.

### Concepts related to runtime fragments

- For module identity and root filenames see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For builder slots and command policy see @#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
- For routes and synthetic single-file pages see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).
